WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.930
If a film earns widespread critical acclaim
but audiences don't turn out to watch it in

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:06.800
theaters, is it still worth your time?

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.680
That's a question that's been posed plenty
of times in response to the lackluster performances

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:14.940
of tentpole films like Blade Runner 2049 and
Solo.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.020
In order to try to answer that question, let's
take a look into the past.

00:00:19.227 --> 00:00:20.767
Scott Pilgrim vs. The World

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:26.867
In 2010, Scott Pilgrim vs. The World hit screens
with both the ultraviolence of a Quentin Tarantino

00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:29.387
movie and the sensibilities of a classic video
game.

00:00:30.007 --> 00:00:34.007
With Arrested Development's Michael Cera playing
the film's quirky hero, it felt as if the

00:00:34.017 --> 00:00:36.187
movie was destined for box office greatness.

00:00:36.187 --> 00:00:36.947
Instead…

00:00:41.707 --> 00:00:44.427
"If I peed my pants would you pretend I just
got wet from the rain?"

00:00:44.427 --> 00:00:45.507
"It's not raining."

00:00:45.507 --> 00:00:46.007
"Oh."

00:00:46.007 --> 00:00:51.366
Universal Pictures poured a reported $60 million
into the film and stars like Mary Elizabeth

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:55.687
Winstead, Anna Kendrick, and future Captain
America Chris Evans rounded out the cast,

00:00:56.116 --> 00:00:58.407
but none of that helped the studio earn back
its money.

00:00:58.407 --> 00:01:03.707
Bringing in a mere $31 million domestically,
the movie did manage to win over the critics,

00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:06.047
with Peter Travers of Rolling Stone raving,

00:01:06.147 --> 00:01:10.807
"Scott Pilgrim is a breathless rush of a movie
that jumps off the screen, spins your head

00:01:10.807 --> 00:01:13.347
around and then stealthily works its way into
your heart."

00:01:13.827 --> 00:01:16.227
So why didn't more people rush to theaters
to see it?

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:20.687
You can probably chalk it up to a combination
of poor marketing, the still growing comic-book

00:01:20.687 --> 00:01:24.727
movie formula, and the not-quite universal
star-power of Michael Cera.

00:01:25.607 --> 00:01:26.527
Man on the Moon

00:01:27.407 --> 00:01:31.647
In the late '90s, Jim Carrey was, without
a doubt, at the top of Hollywood's food chain.

00:01:32.247 --> 00:01:36.737
With the success of films like Ace Ventura,
Liar Liar, and The Mask under his belt, it

00:01:36.737 --> 00:01:38.367
seemed as if he could do no wrong.

00:01:38.487 --> 00:01:43.006
And when it was announced he would be bringing
the life story of legendary comic Andy Kaufman

00:01:43.006 --> 00:01:47.117
to the big screen, it felt like a no-brainer
that the rubber-faced, often-misunderstood

00:01:47.117 --> 00:01:50.367
comedian would do a great job paying tribute
to one of his heroes.

00:01:50.647 --> 00:01:54.427
Unfortunately, Man on the Moon wasn't the
hit the studio had been hoping for.

00:01:54.427 --> 00:01:57.807
As long as Carrey was delivering belly laughs,
audiences came flocking.

00:01:58.027 --> 00:02:03.327
But, much like with his turn in the Ben Stiller-directed
dark comedy The Cable Guy, fans weren't sure

00:02:03.327 --> 00:02:05.967
how to react to a more dramatic turn from
the actor.

00:02:06.387 --> 00:02:11.307
The movie only earned a little more than half
of its budget, taking in a measly $34 million.

00:02:11.687 --> 00:02:15.607
While Universal took the hit for that loss,
the film was celebrated by critics.

00:02:15.607 --> 00:02:20.657
And, as seen in Netflix's documentary Jim
&amp; Andy, Carrey went completely method in bringing

00:02:20.657 --> 00:02:22.407
Kaufman to life, and it worked for him.

00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:27.827
Box office woes aside, it won Carrey a Golden
Globe and helped pave the way for more serious

00:02:27.827 --> 00:02:28.327
roles.

00:02:29.207 --> 00:02:30.187
The Hurt Locker

00:02:30.947 --> 00:02:35.357
Before they started protecting the world as
Hawkeye and Falcon in the Avengers movies,

00:02:35.357 --> 00:02:39.407
Jeremy Renner and Anthony Mackie partnered
up in Kathryn Bigelow's The Hurt Locker.

00:02:39.807 --> 00:02:44.857
Released in 2009, this gritty look at America's
military in Iraq not only made Bigelow the

00:02:44.857 --> 00:02:49.487
first woman to win a Best Director Oscar,
but it also put a more modern spin on the

00:02:49.487 --> 00:02:50.447
war movie formula.

00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:56.267
But as numerous timely war films have discovered,
it's always a challenge to bring in mass viewership

00:02:56.267 --> 00:03:00.077
when the subject matter is a very real, and
very recent, armed conflict.

00:03:00.667 --> 00:03:05.127
When it came to theatrical revenues, The Hurt
Locker ended up being a clear loser in the

00:03:05.127 --> 00:03:10.017
box office battle, barely making back its
$15 million budget domestically in the year

00:03:10.017 --> 00:03:15.336
that found Avatar, Harry Potter and the Half
Blood Prince, Up, and Paramount's Star Trek

00:03:15.336 --> 00:03:17.647
reboot all vying for audiences' attention.

00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:21.427
There's something to be said for the sheer
mindless joy of escapism that those movies

00:03:21.427 --> 00:03:26.807
offer, and, unfortunately for Bigelow's acclaimed
effort, viewers were more inclined to revisit

00:03:26.807 --> 00:03:28.067
Hogwarts than the Middle East.

00:03:29.707 --> 00:03:30.207
Hugo

00:03:31.007 --> 00:03:35.847
Martin Scorsese took a break from his usual
sort of movie in 2011 with this kid-friendly

00:03:35.847 --> 00:03:36.347
fantasy.

00:03:36.847 --> 00:03:41.577
The story followed Asa Butterfield's Hugo
as he unravels a mystery through steam-powered

00:03:41.577 --> 00:03:46.287
riddles and historical clips of early 20th-century
cinema, and it was breathtaking.

00:03:46.927 --> 00:03:51.127
During its theatrical run, however, the movie
didn't even come close to breaking the bank,

00:03:51.647 --> 00:03:57.847
taking in $181 million worldwide, and barely
earning back a staggering $180 million budget.

00:03:58.097 --> 00:04:03.527
Hugo still earned a total of 11 Oscar nominations
that year, adding to the director's long history

00:04:03.527 --> 00:04:05.007
of critically acclaimed films.

00:04:05.447 --> 00:04:07.927
So why didn't Hugo bring in the box office
bucks?

00:04:08.107 --> 00:04:09.627
One word: competition.

00:04:10.147 --> 00:04:15.427
The movie arrived just one week after The
Twilight Saga: Breaking Dawn, Part One, and

00:04:15.427 --> 00:04:16.507
Disney's new Muppets movie.

00:04:17.137 --> 00:04:22.187
Basically, the combination of sparkly vampires
and Kermit the Frog was too much for Hugo

00:04:22.187 --> 00:04:22.687
to handle.

